Create a premium vertical food-fashion campaign built around an exaggerated scale contrast: one enormous food product becomes an architectural stage, while the photorealistic model appears much smaller and interacts naturally with it.

The product should occupy roughly 50–65% of the frame and remain the main visual focus. Make its texture highly realistic and appetizing, with material details appropriate to the selected product.

Design the model’s pose around the product’s actual shape and structure. The interaction should feel varied and visually meaningful rather than defaulting to a standard sitting pose. Keep believable weight, contact, perspective, hands, feet, and body proportions.

The model should feel youthful, bright, attractive, and genuinely photorealistic, with natural skin texture, realistic hair, expressive eyes, and a lively presence.

Add one oversized original headline behind the product and model. Give the typography a material treatment inspired by the product itself. Let parts of the letters overlap naturally with the product and model to create spatial depth.

Add 3–5 short pieces of original editorial-style brand copy. Keep the layout asymmetrical, refined, and visually integrated with the composition. Do not use “VOL.1”, “01”, “ISSUE”, decorative numbering, or generic placeholder text.

Use ingredients and product-related props only as secondary foreground or environmental elements. Keep them mainly around the edges and foreground, with selective natural blur to create depth. They should enrich the atmosphere without competing with the giant product and model.

Use bright commercial photography, clean studio lighting, subtle reflections, product-specific atmosphere, realistic materials, polished editorial styling, strong visual hierarchy, and a clear sense of scale.

Prompt:

[STORY MOMENT]: {birthday surprise / first day opening a shop / reading a letter / finding a stray cat / etc.}
[CHARACTER]: {age, gender, appearance, outfit}
[ACTION]: {the exact moment being captured}
[ARCHITECTURAL FRAGMENT]: {doorway / steps / storefront / mailbox wall / corridor / etc.}
[TITLE]: {one short keyword}
[ACCENT COLOR]: {red / blue / orange / etc.}
[SHORT LINE]: {one meaningful sentence}
[SUBTITLE]: {short story label}
[FORMAT]: 9:16

Create a vertical editorial poster built around one small but meaningful moment from everyday life.

Show a realistic adult character naturally caught in the middle of an action, with a readable face, subtle emotion, and no camera awareness. The scene should feel like a real lifestyle photograph with a story already happening inside it, not a posed fashion shoot or commercial ad.

Do not show the entire environment. Keep only the architectural fragments and objects needed to tell the story, such as a doorway, steps, mailbox, shutter, wall, window, bag, cake, letter, records, plants, or other relevant details. Leave generous clean negative space around the main composition.

Set “{TITLE}” in oversized bold condensed typography and integrate it deeply into the physical space. Match the letters to the perspective of walls, doors, stairs, floors, shutters, or other architectural planes. Use strong near-to-far scale changes and clear vanishing directions so the typography feels embedded in the environment rather than placed flat over the image.

Let the character, objects, door frames, railings, furniture, or architectural edges overlap and interrupt parts of the letters. Keep the title visually powerful but make the story moment the emotional focus.

Use an off-white, cool gray, concrete gray, or muted neutral base with subtle paper and print texture. Avoid strong yellow casts, sepia tones, dirty grunge, or overly warm vintage filters. Keep skin tones natural and clean. Use {ACCENT COLOR} only in a few small graphic details.

Include:
TYPE FIELD
2026
{TITLE}
{SHORT LINE}
{SUBTITLE}

Keep supporting text minimal and meaningful. Avoid random filler copy, excessive technical data, numbering, logos, watermarks, sparkles, and decorative clutter.

